<h2><a name=3D"t1"></a><a id=3D"_1"></a><font style=3D"vertical-align: inhe=
rit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">background</font></font></h2=
>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">In the development project, I need to use an MPU to drive a 1280x=
1024 dual-channel LVDS LCD screen. Unfortunately, this MPU hardware only su=
pports TTL RGB interface and MIPI interface, and there is no ready-made LVD=
S interface. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">At first, I tr=
ied to use THC63LVD827 to convert TTL RGB into dual-channel LVDS to drive L=
CD screen, but found that the MPU's TTL RGB interface can only drive 1280x7=
68 60fps display at most. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">H=
elpless, we can only start from MIPI to LVDS. The MIPI interface of the MPU=
 can support up to 4lane 1.5Gbps MIPI signal transmission, and can drive up=
 to 1920x1080 60fps display. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;=
">Then I searched for information and found that GM8775C can meet the needs=
 of the author to convert MIPI into dual-channel LVDS to drive a 1280x1024 =
LCD screen.</font></font></p>=20
<h2><a name=3D"t2"></a><a id=3D"GM8775C_3"></a><font style=3D"vertical-alig=
n: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">Introduction of GM877=
5C</font></font></h2>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t3"></a><a id=3D"1_4"></a><font style=3D"vertical-align: inh=
erit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">1. Product overview</font><=
/font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">The GM8775C DSI to dual-channel LVDS transmitter product mainly r=
ealizes the function of converting MIPI DSI to single/dual-channel LVDS. MI=
PI supports 1/2/3/4 channels optional, and supports a maximum rate of 4Gbps=
. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">The LVDS clock frequency =
is up to 154MHz, and the maximum supported video format is FULL HD (1920 x =
1200). </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font sty=
le=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">The chip is mainly used in handheld devices=
, dual-screen display, large-screen display and other application requireme=
nts.</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t4"></a><a id=3D"2_7"></a><font style=3D"vertical-align: inh=
erit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">2. Product Features</font><=
/font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">a) I/0 power supply voltage: 1.8V /3.3V; </font></font><br><font =
style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"=
>b) core power supply voltage: 1.8V; </font></font><br><font style=3D"verti=
cal-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">c) Support MI=
PI=C2=AE D-PHY 1.00.00 and MIPI=C2=AE DSI 1.02.00. </font></font><br><font =
style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"=
>d) MIPI supports 1/2/3/4 channel optional transmission mode, the highest r=
ate is 1Gbps/channel. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inhe=
rit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">e) MIPI accepts 18bpp RGB666=
, 24bpp RGB888, 16bpp RGB565 packaging formats. </font></font><br><font sty=
le=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">f)=
 MIPI supports LPDT transmission (Low-Power Data Transmission) and reverse =
LPDT transmission. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit=
;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">g) The LVDS clock range is 25MH=
z to 154MHz. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><fo=
nt style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">h) LVDS output supports single/dual c=
hannel mode. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">When dual-chan=
nel mode is selected, the output can be configured as 18/24bit, JEIDA/VESA =
mode; when single-channel is selected, each channel can output at the same =
time, and the output mode (18/24bit, JEIDA/VESA mode) can be configured ind=
ependently. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><fon=
t style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">i) The output data channels of LVDS ca=
n be flexibly adjusted in order to facilitate PCB layout. </font></font><br=
><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: in=
herit;">j) Can choose to use MIPI clock or external reference clock as the =
reference frequency of LVDS output, and supports automatic calibration func=
tion. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font styl=
e=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">k) Support MIPI command mode configuration a=
nd external I2C configuration two chip configuration methods; </font></font=
><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align=
: inherit;">l) GPO can output PWM signal to control the screen backlight. <=
/font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"ve=
rtical-align: inherit;">m) Package: QFN48-pins with e-pad. </font></font><b=
r><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: i=
nherit;">n) Operating temperature: -40=E2=84=83~85=E2=84=83; </font></font>=
<br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align:=
 inherit;">o) ESD capability: =E2=89=A52KV.</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t5"></a><a id=3D"3_23"></a><font style=3D"vertical-align: in=
herit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">3. Product functional bloc=
k diagram</font></font></h3>=20
<p><img src=3D"https://img-blog.csdnimg.cn/20210617171008141.png?x-oss-proc=
ess=3Dimage/watermark,type_ZmFuZ3poZW5naGVpdGk,shadow_10,text_aHR0cHM6Ly9ib=
G9nLmNzZG4ubmV0L2xpbnlpeWFuZzg0NzU=3D,size_16,color_FFFFFF,t_70" alt=3D"ins=
ert image description here"></p>=20
<h2><a name=3D"t6"></a><a id=3D"GM8775C_26"></a><font style=3D"vertical-ali=
gn: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">GM8775C debugging</f=
ont></font></h2>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t7"></a><a id=3D"1LVDS_lanePN_27"></a><font style=3D"vertica=
l-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">1. Swap LVDS la=
ne, P/N</font></font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">The GM8775C chip supports LVDS lane switching and P/N switching w=
ithin the same group of lanes, which is convenient for PCB routing. </font>=
<font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">The figure below is the reference =
PCB provided by GM8775C. It can be seen that the LVDS output does not need =
to punch through holes or multiple wires, which is very convenient. </font>=
</font><img src=3D"https://img-blog.csdnimg.cn/20210617171845383.png?x-oss-=
process=3Dimage/watermark,type_ZmFuZ3poZW5naGVpdGk,shadow_10,text_aHR0cHM6L=
y9ibG9nLmNzZG4ubmV0L2xpbnlpeWFuZzg0NzU=3D,size_16,color_FFFFFF,t_70" alt=3D=
"insert image description here" width=3D"400" height=3D"400"><br><font styl=
e=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">If =
the lane or P/N is adjusted, the corresponding options need to be changed w=
hen the host computer is used to generate the configuration register later.=
 </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">The author's own board onl=
y exchanges the LVDS lane, not the P/N, so when the host computer is used t=
o test the back end of the GM8775 to drive the LVDS LCD screen, it needs to=
 be adjusted as follows. </font></font><br> <img src=3D"https://img-blog.cs=
dnimg.cn/20210617172657547.png?x-oss-process=3Dimage/watermark,type_ZmFuZ3p=
oZW5naGVpdGk,shadow_10,text_aHR0cHM6Ly9ibG9nLmNzZG4ubmV0L2xpbnlpeWFuZzg0NzU=
=3D,size_16,color_FFFFFF,t_70" alt=3D"insert image description here"><br><f=
ont style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inher=
it;">The host computer is used to generate the GM8775C configuration regist=
er, and can pass the usb Turn to the IIC interface to configure and test th=
e GM8775C function.</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t8"></a><a id=3D"226MhzIICBIST_TEST_33"></a><font style=3D"v=
ertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">2. Use ex=
ternal 26Mhz crystal oscillator, IIC command for BIST TEST</font></font></h=
3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">At the beginning, in order to eliminate the interference of other=
 factors, the author first used the external 26Mhz crystal oscillator as th=
e clock source of the GM8775C, and used the usb to iic tool to BIST TEST it=
. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">The I2C_TYPE pin of GM877=
5C needs to be pulled low as Slave, I2C_ADDR is selected to be pulled high,=
 and the device address is 0x5A; if it is selected to be pulled low, the de=
vice address is 0x58. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inhe=
rit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">After configuring the corres=
ponding parameters, click to generate register parameters, and select the c=
orresponding device address to write data. </font><font style=3D"vertical-a=
lign: inherit;">If there is no problem after the test, the screen can be di=
splayed normally. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">If it is =
not lit, read back the data to compare to see if the data is written correc=
tly. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">If the read and write =
data are consistent, check whether the configuration is correct, whether th=
e crystal oscillator starts to vibrate, and whether the chip is soldered pr=
operly.</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t9"></a><a id=3D"326MhzMIPIBIST_TEST_36"></a><font style=3D"=
vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">3. Use e=
xternal 26Mhz crystal oscillator, MIPI command for BIST TEST</font></font><=
/h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">After the above test is no problem, it can be confirmed that the =
back-end output lvds signal is correct, and the back-end circuit is no prob=
lem. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">Then reset the GM8775C=
, copy the generated register set data to the mpu, let the mpu generate the=
 MIPI signal, and send the configuration register data from lane0 to the GM=
8775C through MIPI to see if it can be powered on normally. </font><font st=
yle=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">If it can be lit, it proves that there is =
no problem with the clock and lane0 line sequence of the mipi signal, and t=
he communication at low speed is normal; if it cannot be lit, then there is=
 a problem with the welding or MIPI line sequence.</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t10"></a><a id=3D"4MIPIMIPIBIST_TEST_38"></a><font style=3D"=
vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">4. Use M=
IPI clock signal, MIPI command for BIST TEST</font></font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">After there is no problem in the test, MIPI can be used to provid=
e the clock source for GM8775C, which can save a 26Mhz crystal oscillator a=
nd test whether MIPI works normally at high speed. </font><font style=3D"ve=
rtical-align: inherit;">If it cannot be displayed normally, you need to che=
ck whether the wiring of the mipi meets the specifications.</font></font></=
p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t11"></a><a id=3D"5MIPIMIPI_40"></a><font style=3D"vertical-=
align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">5. Use MIPI clock=
 signal, MIPI command for application test</font></font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">If there is no problem, you can test the signals of all MIPI line=
s, configure the MIPI Clock frequency, select MIPI Clock as the clock sourc=
e, MIPI command as the configuration command, close BIST TEST, generate reg=
ister configuration data, write to MPU, Start application testing. </font><=
/font><br> <img src=3D"https://img-blog.csdnimg.cn/20210617181409962.png?x-=
oss-process=3Dimage/watermark,type_ZmFuZ3poZW5naGVpdGk,shadow_10,text_aHR0c=
