<h3><a name=3D"t15"></a><a id=3D"2MIPI_52"></a><font style=3D"vertical-alig=
n: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">2. MIPI wiring</font>=
</font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">MIPI is a high-speed differential signal interface, which has str=
ict wiring requirements. In order to ensure the synchronization and consist=
ency of signals, it is necessary to ensure that the P/N lines of the same g=
roup of MIPI signals are of the same length. </font><font style=3D"vertical=
-align: inherit;">In the LCD MIPI signal lines, the length difference betwe=
en groups is not more than 200mil, and the difference between the P/N signa=
l lines of each group is not more than 60mil. </font><font style=3D"vertica=
l-align: inherit;">The length should be as short as possible. </font></font=
><br><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align=
: inherit;">At the same time, it is necessary to make as few holes as possi=
ble to change layers, keep away from interference as much as possible, and =
ensure a continuous reference layer when routing, and preferably the ground=
 layer, and the differential impedance is required to be 100ohm. </font><fo=
nt style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">Other more requirements can refer to =
other articles on the Internet. </font></font><br><font style=3D"vertical-a=
lign: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">At the beginning, =
the author kept the MIPI to LVDS function and the TTL RGB interface for the=
 MPU to be able to drive both LVDS and TTL RGB screens. As a result, the wi=
ring was not easy to go, and the MIPI layout was not very good, and the LCD=
 screen could not be driven later, so Try to simplify the layout of MIPI si=
gnal lines to meet the above wiring requirements and reduce unnecessary tro=
ubles.</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t16"></a><a id=3D"3GM8775C_56"></a><font style=3D"vertical-a=
lign: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">3. GM8775C welding=
</font></font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">During the welding process, the GM8775C and MPU must be welded we=
ll, especially the MIPI pin position. When testing the application driver s=
creen, all MIPI signals must be normally transmitted to the GM8775C to see =
normal images. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">When the aut=
hor tests the back-end LVDS signal on the GM8775C host computer alone, the =
test is good, but the signal driver transferred by MIPI is not good, and th=
e wiring is also very good. </font><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"=
>After pressing the GM8775C by hand, it actually lights up, which is obviou=
sly poor soldering contact. I once thought that there was a problem with th=
e software design or the MIPI wiring was not good! </font><font style=3D"ve=
rtical-align: inherit;">Really fucked up!</font></font></p>=20
<h3><a name=3D"t17"></a><a id=3D"4win10usbi2c_58"></a><font style=3D"vertic=
al-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;">4. Win10 syste=
m cannot download even-numbered line configuration using usb to i2c</font><=
/font></h3>=20
<p><font style=3D"vertical-align: inherit;"><font style=3D"vertical-align: =
inherit;">After using the host computer to generate the register configurat=
ion, the author used the usb to i2c tool to download the configuration on t=
he win10 system and found that the configuration of writing and then readin=
g the even-numbered lines was different from that of the writing. I used a =
logic analyzer to capture the i2c data and found that it was not written. T=
he register configuration items of the even-numbered lines are the same for=
 both win10 systems, but they are normal on win7 systems. I don't know if t=
his is a usb driver problem or a host computer problem.</font></font></p>=
=20
